atauranga me te rangahau\nL76: \nL77: 【30†Enrolment statistics】\nL78: \nL79: 【31†Participation in voting】\nL80: \nL81: 【32†Applications for roll data】\nL82: \nL83: 【33†MMP seat allocation calculator】\nL84: \nL85: 【34†Party donations and loans】\nL86: \nL87: 【35†Election reports】\nL88: \nL89: 【36†Election results†electionresults.govt.nz】\nL90: 【5†About us】 【6†Media & news】 【7†Jobs†work.elections.nz】 【8†Contact】\nL91: \nL92: 【37†Party Portal Login】\nL93: \nL94: [Button: In this section]\nL95: \nL96: 【9†What is New Zealand’s system of government?】\nL97: \nL98:   * 【38†What is MMP?】\nL99:   * How are MPs elected?\nL100:   * 【39†Report of the Royal Commission on the Electoral System 1986】\nL101: \nL102: Your two votes help decide which candidates get the 120 seats in Parliament and become members of Parliament (MPs).\nL103: Candidates with the most electorate votes in each voting area become electorate MPs. Candidates on a party list can become list MPs if their party gets at least 5% of the party vote or wins one electorate.\nL104: \nL105: A party's total number of electorate and list MPs will be about the same as its share of the party vote.\nL106: ## Electing an electorate MP\nL107: \nL108: The candidate with the most votes in your electorate becomes your electorate MP. They do not need more than half the votes.\nL109: \nL110: Your electorate MP will represent your local area in Parliament.\nL111: ## Electing parties into Parliament\nL112: \nL113: If a party gets at least 5% of the party vote or wins an electorate, we use the Sainte-Laguë formula to decide how many seats it gets.\nL114: \nL115: Each party gets a share of seats that's close to its share of the party vote. For example, a party with 30% of the party vote gets about 36 seats - 30% of the 120 seats in Parliament.\nL116: ### Who gets a party's seats\nL117: \nL118: A party's seats first go to their candidates that won electorates. Any leftover seats go to candidates from the party list, in the order the party ranks them.\nL119: \nL120: For example, a party wins 30% of the party vote and 20 of their candidates win electorates. Twenty of their 36 seats go to the winning electorate candidates. The other 16 seats go to candidates from its party list.\nL121: ### If a party wins more electorates than its share of the party vote\nL122: \nL123: A party can win more electorates than seats it's entitled to from its share of the party vote. The party keeps the extra seats, creating an overhang. The size of Parliament increases by the number of extra seats until the next general election. An overhang does not affect the number of seats other parties get.\nL124: For example, a party with 5% of the party vote would normally get about six seats. If that party also wins seven electorates, it gets seven electorate MPs and no list MPs. The total number of MPs increases to 121.\nL125: \nL126: New Zealand has had five Parliaments with overhangs.\nL127:   * From 2005 to 2008, there were 121 MPs\nL128:   * From 2008 to 2011, there were 122 MPs\nL129:   * From 2011 to 2014, there were 121 MPs\nL130:   * From 2014 to 2017, there were 121 MPs\nL131:   * In the current Parliament, following the 2023 General Election, there are 123 MPs\nL132: \nL133: 【1†Back to top】\nL134: \nL135: 【37†Party Portal Login】 【5†About the Electoral Commission】 | 【6†Media & news】 | 【40†Privacy & security】 | 【7†Jobs†work.elections.nz】 | 【8†Contact】\nL136: \nL137: 0800 36 76 56\nL138: 【41†Get in touch via our Facebook†www.facebook.com】 【42†Follow us on Twitter†www.twitter.com】 【43†Follow us on Instagram†www.instagram.com】 【44†Have a look at our YouTube channel†www.youtube.com】 【45†Connect with us on LinkedIn†nz.linkedin.com】\nL139: \nL140: 【4†Electoral Commission】\nL141: \nL142: Copyright ©\nL143: Electoral Commission\nL144: New Zealand\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhat is MMP? | Elections NZ (https://www.elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/what-is-new-zealands-system-of-government/what-is-mmp/)\nciteturn30view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/what-is-new-zealands-system-of-government/what-is-mmp/\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/what-is-new-zealands-system-of-government/what-is-mmp; Total lines: 158\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Skip to main content】 【2†Skip to navigation within this section】\nL3: \nL4: 2026 General Election 【3†Find out more†vote.nz】\nL5: \nL6: Menu\nL7: \nL8: 【4†Go to Homepage】\nL9: \nL10:   * 【5†About us】 |\nL11:   * 【6†Media & news】 |\nL12:   * 【7†Jobs†work.elections.nz】 |\nL13:   * 【8†Contact】\nL14: \nL15: Search site\nL16: \nL17: [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL18: \nL19: Search\nL20: \nL21: [Input: I'm looking for ...]\nL22: \nL23: Search Search\nL24: \nL25: Close search\nL26: \nL27: Main\nL28: \nL29:   * Democracy in NZ Te manapori i Aotearoa\nL30: \nL31: 【9†What is New Zealand’s system of government?】\nL32: 【10†Political parties in New Zealand】\nL33: \nL34: 【11†What is an electorate?】\nL35: \nL36: 【12†What is an electoral roll?】\nL37: \nL38: 【13†About elections】\nL39: \nL40: 【14†Past elections】\nL41: \nL42:   * Guidance and rules Ngā tohutohu me ngā ture\nL43: \nL44: 【15†Candidate Hub】\nL45: \nL46: 【16†For parties】\nL47: \nL48: 【17†For starting a political party】\nL49: \nL50: 【18†For third party promoters】\nL51: \nL52: 【19†Advertising and campaigning】\nL53: \nL54: 【20†Donations and loans】\nL55: \nL56: 【21†Election protocols】\nL57: \nL58:   * Getting involved Te whakauru mai\nL59: \nL60: 【22†Teaching voting at schools】\nL61: \nL62: 【23†Educating adults and communities】\nL63: 【24†How you can get more involved】\nL64: \nL65: 【25†Get brochures, posters and forms】\nL66: \nL67: 【26†Download resources】\nL68: \nL69: 【27†Candidate Hub】\nL70: \nL71: 【28†Election Access Fund】\nL72: \nL73: 【29†Community Education Fund】\nL74: \nL75:   * Stats and research Ngā tatauranga me te rangahau\nL76: \nL77: 【30†Enrolment statistics】\nL78: \nL79: 【31†Participation in voting】\nL80: \nL81: 【32†Applications for roll data】\nL82: \nL83: 【33†MMP seat allocation calculator】\nL84: \nL85: 【34†Party donations and loans】\nL86: \nL87: 【35†Election reports】\nL88: \nL89: 【36†Election results†electionresults.govt.nz】\nL90: 【5†About us】 【6†Media & news】 【7†Jobs†work.elections.nz】 【8†Contact】\nL91: \nL92: 【37†Party Portal Login】\nL93: \nL94: [Button: In this section]\nL95: \nL96: 【9†What is New Zealand’s system of government?】\nL97:   * What is MMP?\nL98:     * 【38†25 years of MMP】\nL99:     * 【39†The history of MMP】\nL100:     * 【40†A Royal Commission and two referendums】\nL101:     * 【41†The introduction of MMP】\nL102:     * 【42†A more diverse Parliament】\nL103:     * 【43†The Electoral Commission】\nL104:     * 【44†2012 MMP Review】\nL105:   * 【45†How are MPs elected?】\nL106:   * 【46†Report of the Royal Commission on the Electoral System 1986】\nL107: \nL108: MMP is the voting system we use in New Zealand. It stands for ‘Mixed Member Proportional’. We use MMP to choose who represents us in Parliament.\nL109: 【47†iframe†www.youtube-nocookie.com】\nL110: \nL111: Parliament has 120 seats for its members of Parliament (MPs). During an election, political parties try to win as many seats in Parliament as they can.\nL112: ## You have two votes under MMP\nL113: \nL114: Under MMP, you get two votes — a party vote and an electorate vote.\nL115: \nL116: 【48†Image: The words ‘Each voter has a party vote and an electorate vote’, above a graphic of a hand putting a vote in a ballot box.】\nL117: ### You vote for the political party you want to represent you\nL118: \nL119: When you vote for a party, you help to choose how many seats in Parliament each party gets.\nL120: \nL121: The party vote largely decides the total number of seats each political party gets. Parties with a bigger share of the party vote get more seats in Parliament. Parties also try to win as many electorate seats as possible.\nL122: ### You vote for the candidate you want to represent the area you live in\nL123: \nL124: When you vote for a candidate, you help to choose who represents the electorate you live in. This is called your electorate vote. The candidate with the most votes wins, and becomes an MP.\nL125: ## Political parties try to win party votes and electorate votes\nL126: \nL127: Political parties must get at least 5% of the party vote or win an electorate seat before they can have any seats in Parliament.\nL128: \nL129: Because MMP is a proportional system, the share of seats a party wins in Parliament is about the same as its share of the party vote. This applies to big parties and small parties.\nL130: \nL131: 【49†Image: A pie chart representing the party vote is divided into the same proportions as a graphic of the seats in parliament.】\nL132: ### How seats in Parliament are allocated\nL133: \nL134: Every candidate who wins an electorate gets a seat in Parliament. They are called electorate MPs.\nL135: \nL136: The remaining seats are filled from party lists. Every party has a party list, which is a list of candidates ranked in the order the party wants those candidates to be elected to Parliament. Candidates elected from a party list are called list MPs.\nL137: ## Parties negotiate to form a government\nL138: \nL139: Usually no party gets enough votes to govern alone. Parties often need to come to an agreement with other parties to form a government or pass legislation.\nL140: \nL141: Some types of possible agreements are:\nL142: \nL143:   * coalitions — when two or more parties join together to form a government\nL144:   * confidence and supply agreements — when one party agrees to support another on certain issues and laws that are voted on in Parliament.\nL145: \nL146: 【1†Back to top】\nL147: 【37†Party Portal Login】 【5†About the Electoral Commission】 | 【6†Media & news】 | 【50†Privacy & security】 | 【7†Jobs†work.elections.nz】 | 【8†Contact】\nL148: